restriction: please write the implementation summary inside the task {...} block, not outside!

task (completed) {
  in task.openlibrary_search, when searching keywords like "the rust programming language", the result should contain all the books that are around the whole "the rust programming language"

  please make a systematic change

  Implemented:
  app/chat_server.py
  - /openlibrary search now queries both Open Library's full-text
    (search/inside) endpoint and its catalogue (search.json) endpoint in
    parallel. The full-text endpoint matches query words independently, so
    "the rust programming language" returned books about rust the metal or
    the fungal disease; the catalogue endpoint matches the whole title/author
    phrase and contributes works such as "The Rust Programming Language".
  - Added _openlibrary_doc_card() to normalize catalogue works, and
    _combine_openlibrary_cards() to merge catalogue and full-text cards by
    work key, deduplicate, and rank them against the complete query phrase.
  - Relevance now scores title/author coverage and an exact title phrase
    highest. Excerpt relevance uses the best single passage that covers the
    query terms, not terms scattered across unrelated passages, so a book that
    merely mentions "rust", "programming", and "language" in separate
    excerpts no longer outranks a book about the whole phrase.
  - The result limit is 20 (catalogue candidates are fetched wider), so all
    works around the queried phrase are shown rather than being cut off by
    loose full-text matches. Work descriptions are still enriched in parallel
    and the existing Open Library card UI is unchanged.

  Verified:
  - /openlibrary search "the rust programming language" returns The Rust
    Programming Language first, followed by its other editions and related
    Rust language books such as Best Practices of Rust Programming Language,
    Rust Web Programming, Rust Cookbook, and Rust Standard Library Cookbook.
  - /openlibrary search "rust programming language" also ranks the Rust
    language books first and returns 20 cards.
  - py_compile passes for app/chat_server.py.
}

task.openlibrary_search (completed) {
  create new command: /openlibrary search <keywords>
  example:
  https://openlibrary.org/search/inside.json?q=rust+borrow+checker

  learn from the current UI/UX of /news earch <keywords>

  in the search result, i would like to have:
  1. the book title/url/description
  2. the related text

  Implemented:
  app/chat_server.py
  - Added /openlibrary search <keywords>. It calls Open Library's full-text
  inside-search endpoint
  (https://openlibrary.org/search/inside.json?q=<keywords>) and normalizes
  each hit into a book card with title, authors, Open Library URL, catalogue
  metadata, a description, and up to three matched passages from the
  highlight snippets. Duplicate editions of the same work are merged.
  - Descriptions come from the work record when Open Library has one. They are
  fetched in parallel (cached in memory) so the search itself stays fast; when
  no catalogue description exists, a metadata description is used instead.
  - Added /openlibrary to the command list, argument completion, /help, and the
  autocomplete handler for its "search" subcommand.
  app/chat.html
  - Reused the /gutenberg book-card layout and the /news-style command result
  flow. Each card shows the title, authors, description, related full-text
  passages, and a link to the Open Library work page. Unlike /gutenberg cards,
  these cards open the source page in a new tab because the books are not
  downloaded into the local reader.
  - Added "Open Library" as the command entry label and Tab completion for
  /openlibrary search.

  Verified:
  - `search_openlibrary_inside("rust borrow checker", limit=3)` returns title,
  authors, description, Open Library URL, and related-text passages for each
  book.
  - The command emits the same `books` SSE event used by /gutenberg, and the
  usage message is shown when "search <keywords>" is missing.
  - `py_compile` passes for app/chat_server.py and `node --check` passes for the
  browser script.
}
